---
export const prerender = true

import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";
import DocCode from "../../components/docs/DocCode.astro";

const toc = [
  { title: "Overview", href: "#overview", depth: 2 },
  { title: "How it works", href: "#how", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Install", href: "#install", depth: 2 },
  { title: "State file contract", href: "#contract", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Permission reply from the notch", href: "#permissions", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Disable & rollback", href: "#disable", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Source anchors", href: "#source", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Island"
  subtitle="@nikcli-ai/island — NikcliIsland.app, the macOS Dynamic-Island-style notch HUD for live nikcli sessions."
  toc={toc}
  description="The @nikcli-ai/island Swift package and the bridge that mirrors nikcli session state into the file contract the notch app reads."
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      <code>@nikcli-ai/island</code> (the Swift package under
      <code>packages/nikcli-island/</code>) ships <code>NikcliIsland.app</code> — a
      Dynamic-Island-style status HUD that draws over the macOS notch and shows the live
      state of every <code>nikcli</code> session running on the machine. It is modeled on
      <a href="https://github.com/eyadhammouda/pookify" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pookify</a>
      (the equivalent for Claude Code), rebuilt around nikcli's own event bus instead of a
      one-way hook file.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unlike every other package on the docs site, <code>@nikcli-ai/island</code> is a
      Swift package — it does not participate in the JS build graph. Its only dependency
      on nikcli itself is the file contract documented below.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="macOS only">
      Island is a no-op on Windows and Linux. The native app is a macOS-only notch UI. The
      <code>NIKCLI_ISLAND_TEST_FORCE_DARWIN=1</code> flag exists only for the bridge's own
      test suite and must never be set in production.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="how" class="doc-section">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Three moving parts. The data flows from nikcli's process to the notch UI through a
      small JSON file contract that the Swift app polls.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The bridge.</strong>
        <code>packages/nikcli/src/plugin/island/bridge.ts</code> is started unconditionally
        from <code>src/index.ts</code>'s top-level middleware. It listens on
        <code>GlobalBus</code> (<code>src/bus/global.ts</code>), which every
        <code>Bus.publish(...)</code> call already forwards to regardless of mode — so the
        island reflects any nikcli process running on the machine, not just the TUI.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The snapshot file.</strong> One JSON file per session under
        <code>~/Library/Application Support/NikcliIsland/state.d/</code>, written
        atomically (temp file + rename). Each snapshot carries the session id, agent
        title, project, cwd, the writing process's pid, the local server port (when one
        is running), and a server URL.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Swift app.</strong> <code>NikcliIsland.app</code> reads the snapshot
        directory (port of Pookify's <code>SessionAggregator.swift</code>) and draws the
        notch (<code>IslandView.swift</code>, <code>NotchWindow.swift</code>,
        <code>IslandShape.swift</code>, <code>NSScreen+Notch.swift</code>).
      </li>
    </ol>
    <DocCallout type="success" title="Bidirectional — not just display">
      Because the snapshot also carries the writer's local server port and URL, the
      island can call back into <code>POST /permission/:requestID/reply</code> to approve
      or deny a permission request from the notch itself. Pookify can only display
      permission state; the nikcli island can actually answer it.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <p>
      Build the Swift package and drop the app into <code>/Applications</code>:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Terminal">
      # Inside the nikcli monorepo
      ./packages/nikcli-island/scripts/install.sh
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      The <code>install.sh</code> script runs <code>swift build -c release</code>, assembles
      <code>NikcliIsland.app</code>, and installs it to <code>/Applications</code>. An
      <code>uninstall.sh</code> script is shipped alongside it.
    </p>
    <p>To enable the bridge, start any nikcli command with the <code>--island</code> flag:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Terminal">
      nikcli --island
      nikcli tui --island
      nikcli serve --island
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      The first session activity wakes the app via
      <code>open -g -b com.nikcli.island</code>; subsequent publishes are no-ops.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contract" class="doc-section">
    <h2>State file contract</h2>
    <p>
      Each <code>*.json</code> file under <code>~/Library/Application Support/NikcliIsland/state.d/</code>
      is one session's snapshot. The Swift app polls the directory and renders the union.
      The schema is owned by <code>packages/nikcli-island/Sources/IslandCore/</code> on the
      read side and mirrored in <code>src/plugin/island/bridge.ts</code> on the write side.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Field</th>
          <th>Source</th>
          <th>Display</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>idle</code></td>
          <td><code>session.status</code> → <code>idle</code></td>
          <td>Empty pill on the notch.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>thinking</code></td>
          <td><code>session.status</code> → <code>busy</code> / <code>retry</code></td>
          <td>"Thinking…" or "Retrying (N)…".</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>tool</code></td>
          <td><code>message.part.updated</code> tool status</td>
          <td>
            Friendly label plus file basename. Lingers for ~1.9s after completion so the
            last action stays visible.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>permission</code></td>
          <td><code>permission.asked</code></td>
          <td>"Awaiting permission" plus the request id for one-tap approval from the notch.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>error</code></td>
          <td><code>session.error</code></td>
          <td>"Error" pill.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>done</code></td>
          <td>(reserved)</td>
          <td>Used by the native app to mark a completed row before archival.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Beyond the state, each snapshot also carries the parent session id, agent title,
      project, cwd, the writing process pid, the local server port (when one is running),
      and a server URL — enough for the native app to invoke
      <code>POST /permission/:requestID/reply</code> directly when you approve a pending
      request from the notch.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Atomic writes">
      The bridge writes snapshots with the standard temp-file-then-rename idiom so a half-
      written file is never observable by the polling app. See
      <code>IslandBridge.write</code> in <code>src/plugin/island/bridge.ts</code>.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="permissions" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Permission reply from the notch</h2>
    <p>
      The whole reason nikcli's island is more useful than Pookify's is the
      bidirectional channel. Pookify's hook is one-way: it can render
      <code>permission.asked</code>, but it has no way to answer it. nikcli's island can,
      because the writing process is a live nikcli with a real HTTP API and a known
      server URL.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Terminal">{`# What the island POSTs when you tap "Allow" on the notch
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4096/permission/per_example/reply \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"reply":"once"}'`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      The reply handler is the same one the TUI and mobile composer use; see
      <a href="/docs/permissions">Permissions</a> for the full request / reply lifecycle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="disable" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Disable & rollback</h2>
    <p>
      The bridge is opt-in per invocation. To disable the bridge without uninstalling the
      native app, set the environment variable:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Terminal">
      NIKCLI_ISLAND_DISABLE=1 nikcli
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      Toggling from inside the TUI uses the <code>island</code> feature plugin, which
      calls <code>IslandBridge.setEnabled()</code>. Turning it off clears the current
      snapshots and stops writing; turning it back on resumes through the already-
      registered listener without any race window.
    </p>
    <p>To uninstall the native app entirely:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Terminal">
      ./packages/nikcli-island/scripts/uninstall.sh
    </DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Don't set NIKCLI_ISLAND_TEST_FORCE_DARWIN">
      The <code>NIKCLI_ISLAND_TEST_FORCE_DARWIN=1</code> flag exists only for the bridge's
      own test suite. Never set it in production: the feature is Windows/Linux no-op by
      design and forcing it on a non-macOS host would silently write macOS-only paths.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="source" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Source anchors</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Concern</th>
          <th>File</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Swift package</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli-island/</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Install / uninstall scripts</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli-island/scripts/install.sh</code>, <code>uninstall.sh</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Snapshot schema (read side)</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli-island/Sources/IslandCore/</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Notch rendering</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli-island/Sources/NikcliIsland/</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Bridge (write side)</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/plugin/island/bridge.ts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>TUI plugin (toggle)</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/feature-plugins/island/index.tsx</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Worker shutdown hook</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/worker.ts</code> → <code>shutdown</code> RPC</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>